"""Screen-specific operating sequences, kept distinct from interpretation guidance."""
SEQUENCE = {
'01-search': 'Enter a known reference, review the grouped results and select the relevant object. Use the available district and type labels to distinguish similar entries. Opening the record establishes the next context for examining its particulars, relationships and source information; it does not automatically add the result to the case or confirm an identity association.',
'01-search-script': 'Start with the spelling available in the original material and examine the returned variants. Preserve the original name when discussing the source, even if the search used another script. After opening a candidate record, compare the identifiers and context that distinguish that individual. If the enquiry concerns whether two existing records should be associated, continue through the matching review rather than relying on search similarity alone.',
'01-open-case': 'Complete the case title, purpose and basis as separate fields. Include the relevant reference where it is available, then review the form before creating the case. Once it appears in the workspace, inspect its associated records and confirm that it is active. The subsequent dossier, graph and export actions can then be understood against the same recorded investigation context.',
'02-overview-crime': 'Refresh the queue when checking for available updates, then use the category and text filters to isolate the work required. Open an item to examine the associated material. The case panel remains a route to the board and broader analytical views when the update needs to be considered alongside the rest of the investigation.',
'02-overview-supervisor': 'Select the pending request and examine its stated purpose with the visible case information. Record the reason before choosing the applicable decision. Review the resulting state so the team can distinguish a completed decision from a request still awaiting attention. Eligibility checks remain part of the action even when the decision is presented directly in the queue.',
'01-light-theme': 'Open the account controls to change the theme, then return to the same case view. Check that the selected identity and case are appropriate for the task before opening records. For a product demonstration, use the appearance control to show an alternative reading environment and the identity picker only when deliberately demonstrating a different role. The two controls serve independent purposes.',
'01-account-menu': 'Read the displayed user, agency and role before using the demonstration picker. After a change, inspect the navigation and queue to understand which responsibilities are now available. In an operational session, use the configured sign-out action when ending work; the demonstration selector should not be presented as the institution’s authentication mechanism.',
'01-role-refused': 'Read the refusal message, then use Back to overview to return to the role’s available work. Confirm the active identity if the screen was opened from a saved or copied link. The appropriate next step depends on the permitted task: an oversight user can review activity through the audit workspace, while an investigator uses the case functions available to their own role.',
'03-case-suspect-open': 'Open the person’s explanation from the case board and review the facts supporting their position. Follow a relevant account or transfer into the dossier or money trail, or use the network to examine its wider context. Return to the case when deciding whether the matter should become a lead. This sequence connects the board’s summary with the actual evidence the officer examined.',
'03-case-leads-board': 'Locate the card corresponding to the enquiry and review its present column. Move it as the work reaches the next supported stage. When authorisation is required, place the lead in the relevant approval state for a supervisor’s decision. After that decision, the team can distinguish authorised follow-up from an enquiry still being examined. Close the lead when its work has reached the recorded end state.',
'03-case-board-5': 'Review the alert list and timeline for the selected case, then open entries relevant to the period under examination. Compare the source record, amount and date where available. Move to the dossier or financial analysis when more detail is needed. The resulting explanation can then describe the sequence of recorded events rather than relying only on their visual proximity in the board.',
'03-case-close': 'Open the close-case action, enter the outcome and confirm only after reviewing the case status. Use the separate hold controls where retention needs an explicit recorded hold or release. Inspect the retention view later to see how the case is classified; a closure confirmation is not itself a purge record.',
'03-case-network': 'Open Network within the case, choose the relevant layout and narrow the relationship layers where necessary. Select a record to inspect its details and expand only the neighbourhood needed for the question. The same case context remains active when returning to the board, evidence or reports, helping the team connect the visual analysis with its case work.',
'03-case-evidence-form': 'Choose the file, provide a clear description and complete the available production or seizure details. Review the relevant device identifiers and order reference before registering the exhibit. After submission, locate the file in the evidence locker and inspect its recorded fingerprint and custody information. This establishes a practical route from receipt of the file to later verification and selection for release.',
'03-case-evidence': 'Select an exhibit from the locker to inspect its details and custody chain. Use Verify when checking the stored bytes and Download when the case workflow requires a copy. Return to the locker after examining the chain, then use the export workflow when material must be assembled for a named recipient.',
'03-case-evidence-exports': 'Choose the required format, enter the recipient and select the release clearance. Review the intended package contents before exporting. The report, evidence files and supporting manifest serve different purposes, so check the output against the recipient’s need. A later export should be reviewed in its own release context rather than assumed to be identical to an earlier package.',
'03-case-reports-list': 'Select the template that matches the reporting purpose and open the resulting draft. Review the initial sections and available case records before inserting additional material. Develop the narrative in the editor, choose which structured fields should print and save the reviewed draft. Use the recipient-specific export when preparing a fixed copy; the report list remains the point of return for other drafts in the case.',
'03-report-editor': 'Begin with the report’s structure and narrative, then insert the records needed to support each part. Use the right-hand panel to select an exhibit, pattern, area search or relationship where relevant. Inspect the inserted block and add the officer’s interpretation beside it. Reorder the blocks into a coherent reading sequence, save the draft and review the assembled content before opening the export controls.',
'03-report-editor-2': 'Select a record block and review its available print options. Include the relevant particulars, accounts, addresses or history, then add an officer’s note where explanation is required. Move the block to the appropriate position in the narrative. Removing it changes the report’s selection without deleting the underlying record from the investigation.',
'03-report-editor-3': 'Check the unsaved state after changing the title, text or block order. Save the work before treating the draft as ready for release. If a competing update is reported, reload the current version and review it before continuing. Discarding a draft is a separate creator action and should be distinguished from removing one block.',
'03-report-export': 'Open the export action after reviewing the draft. Enter the named reader and the appropriate release clearance, then produce the fixed document. Inspect its heading, selected material and handling information before use. If the report needs further changes, revise the editable draft and review the next export as a new version rather than assuming the previously generated copy will update.',
'19-report-page-1': 'Read the case identity and release context first, then follow the report’s sections in order. Where a statement depends on a particular record or exhibit, use its printed reference to identify the supporting material. Compare the officer’s interpretation with those facts when reviewing a conclusion. The document communicates the selected case narrative; it does not expose every record that remains available in the workspace.',
'19-certificate-page-1': 'Locate the certificate within the evidence package and identify the exhibit or material to which it relates. Compare its populated details with the registration record and the relevant source documentation. Resolve incomplete information through the responsible officer’s review before relying on the document. Retain its relationship to the package and manifest so that a later reader can understand which files the reviewed certificate concerns.',
'04-leads-ncb-request': 'Open the lead detail, inspect the supporting finding and choose whether to request a case or dismiss the lead. For a request, supply the title, purpose and basis required for review. The separate supervisor then records the eligible decision. For dismissal, enter the reason explaining the disposition. Either route makes the response to the finding visible to the workflow.',
'04-leads-syndicate': 'Open the approved case from the lead workflow and verify that it is the active context. Examine the now-available party information and inspect the relevant links in the dossier or network. Use the case lead board to coordinate further work and the reporting tools to preserve reviewed facts. The original finding remains a reference for understanding how this enquiry began.',
'05-alerts-filtered': 'Select the relevant typology filter and open an alert from the narrowed list. Review the amount and path presented in the detail, then follow the associated records into tracing or the network. Check the individual transfers in the dossier when chronology matters. The resulting analysis should describe the transaction sequence and its evidence, rather than simply restating the filter or rule label.',
'06-watchlist': 'Choose the watched record and narrow its activity by kind or importance. Open a relevant item to examine the underlying source, then mark it reviewed when considered. The team can continue through the case tools if follow-up is required or stop the watch with a reason when the monitoring question no longer applies.',
'06-watchlist-area': 'Select the place watch and review the area to which it applies. Examine the available updates by their observation type and time, then open the relevant vehicle, phone or associated record. Compare that material with the reason for the watch before taking a case action. Review status helps the team distinguish updates already considered from newly available observations.',
'06-watch-dialog': 'Enter the investigative reason and choose whether the watch starts now or includes the supported historical period. Review the selected entity or area before confirming. The resulting watch becomes available to the case team for activity review. Its future updates should be assessed against the recorded question, and the watch can be stopped with a reason when that question no longer requires monitoring.',
'06-watchlist-search': 'Select the saved search to inspect its name, reason and matching records. Open relevant results for detailed examination, then mark the material reviewed. Use Stop searching when the query is no longer required. Create or refine a search through Explorer when the underlying question changes, so the retained query continues to describe the intended enquiry.',
'07-network-list': 'Open Record list and search, enter a name or reference and select the intended row. Inspect the selected record before expanding or applying an action to it. Multiple selection supports work on several loaded records, while fit, spacing and labels improve the canvas presentation. These controls offer a precise route through a dense graph.',
'07-network-types': 'Choose the layout corresponding to the question: record type for object categories, money flow for transfers or neighbourhood for a selected record. Apply the relevant relationship filters and inspect the resulting connections. Switch views when another perspective is useful, keeping the same source records and permission boundary in mind while interpreting the different arrangements.',
'07-network-analysis': 'Run analysis on the loaded graph and select the structural result relevant to the enquiry. Open the identified group, connector or cycle and examine its records through the inspector. Compare the types of relationships involved before describing the result. If the finding changes the next investigative question, continue into the case workflow with the supporting source references rather than treating the structural label as a conclusion.',
'07-network-community': 'Select the available community or connectivity result and identify its member records. Inspect links within the group and the records joining it to other components. The loaded graph is the analytical scope, including records hidden on the current canvas. Use the evidence behind those links to explain why the structure matters to the case.',
'07-network-money': 'Switch to Money flow, then inspect the account or party at the start of the relevant route. Follow the directed links and compare the represented amounts. Select a component to examine its details and use the dossier when transaction-level evidence is needed. The flow layout provides orientation; the dated records establish the sequence that can be described in the investigation.',
'07-network-inspector': 'Select one record to inspect its particulars or choose two endpoints for a path enquiry. Choose the appropriate method and review each step in the returned route. Expand a neighbourhood only where it helps the question being examined. Hiding or rearranging items changes the view, while the underlying loaded records remain the analytical reference.',
'08-dossier-overview-2': 'Review the assessment alongside the entity’s identifying details. Open the facts supporting an alert or priority before choosing a follow-up action. Use the network, trace or map for further analysis, or create a lead or watch where the case requires it. Each next step should relate to the question supported by the reviewed material.',
'08-dossier-unmask': 'Identify the protected field required for the enquiry and open the available reveal action. Record the reason in the active case context, then review the result of the access decision. If the value remains unavailable, distinguish a permission refusal from a value that was only stored in partial form. Continue using the information actually available.',
'08-dossier-record': 'Review the listed incident and pattern entries for the selected person. Check each reference, recorded role and current status before opening its supporting detail. Where the view includes contacts, tower information or weapon associations, inspect the available identifiers and source context. Use the material in a lead or report only with the explanation needed to distinguish recorded involvement from the officer’s interpretation.',
'08-dossier-financials': 'Choose an account or amount filter to focus the transaction list. Review a relevant period in the summary, then inspect the individual direction, narration and counterparty references. Use Trace onward when the question concerns subsequent movement. Return to the transaction rows to verify the dated evidence behind any route that will be described in the report.',
'08-dossier-network': 'Open the Network tab for the selected entity and inspect the immediate relationships. Choose a linked record to view its available detail and compare it with the financial, communication or source information in the dossier. Use broader network exploration when the question extends beyond this local context, while retaining the case and the identity under examination.',
'08-dossier-comms': 'Identify a contact relevant to the enquiry and review its count, duration and first and last recorded interaction. Compare the hourly distribution with the period under investigation, then inspect the associated subscriber or identifier where available. Relate any significant timing to the timeline and other source material before including it in an account of the person’s activity.',
'08-dossier-assets': 'Select the journey or asset relevant to the case and inspect its date, route, booking reference or ownership detail. Establish which person the source associates with the item, including family context where shown. Compare the record with the timeline or other evidence before making an interpretation about use or control. The combined tab helps identify relevant material while preserving the different meanings of its source types.',
'08-dossier-timeline': 'Review the latest events or page to the period relevant to the investigation. Open a significant entry to inspect the source from which it was derived. Compare the event with related financial, travel or communication records, then return to the chronological view to assess the sequence. Keep date semantics and source coverage explicit when preparing the narrative.',
'08-dossier-sources': 'Locate the property or source row relevant to the question. Review the source system, reference and available ingestion or matching information. Compare the original material with the consolidated value, particularly if another source disagrees. The resulting assessment should identify whether the value itself, its association with the entity or the interpretation of the record requires further review.',
'09-money-in': 'Select the starting record and choose the inward direction. Set the hop and minimum-amount limits, then inspect the available routes leading to the endpoint. Compare party and account presentations where the distinction matters. Open the relevant counterparties and transactions before describing an origin, keeping the selected limits and source coverage attached to that explanation.',
'09-money-accounts': 'Choose account-level presentation and identify the instrument relevant to the enquiry. Follow its available incoming or outgoing links and inspect the holder information separately. Comparing this view with party aggregation helps distinguish movements between a person’s accounts from movement between different parties. Use the transaction references to support the route described in the case.',
'10-connect-start': 'Select the starting record and inspect the available associates. If the enquiry concerns another specific object, choose that endpoint through the search or related-record controls. Review the resulting relationship view and open relevant dossiers for detail. Clear the second endpoint when returning to a broader examination around the original record.',
'10-connect': 'Check both selected endpoints before reading the connection between them. Follow the chain in order, opening intermediate records where their identity or relationship requires explanation. Distinguish direct interactions from paths through other objects. When the chain informs a case lead, state which links are supported, what time period they concern and which part of the interpretation still requires further evidence.',
'11-globe-zoom': 'Navigate to the region or locality required and inspect the clusters or individual items that become visible. Select a record to open its contextual panel, then follow the available dossier, trace or network action. If a precise local question is required, use an area search with a defined radius and period rather than inferring it from the zoom level.',
'11-area-panel': 'Choose the centre using the available place, camera, tower or map controls. Set the radius, start and end times, and record the reason. Run the enquiry and review both observations and coverage information. Retain these parameters when selecting material for the case so another reviewer can understand which place and period the result represents.',
'11-area-results': 'Review vehicle, phone and travel observations separately, then compare their recorded times and locations. Open an owner or associated dossier when identity context is needed. Use the network to examine relationships relevant to the enquiry. Where the result informs a report or watch, preserve the search area and time interval so the later reader can understand the boundaries of the original question.',
'11-area-results-2': 'Inspect the observation rows and their source indicators before following an associated person or object. Compare registered-resident information separately from timed observations. Where the view indicates missing source coverage, retain that limitation in the analysis. Continue through the relevant dossier or network only when the result provides a supported basis for further examination.',
'12-explore': 'Choose the relevant incident dimension or enter a direct record search. Selecting a summary narrows the underlying results, which can then be inspected individually. Review properties and provenance before adding material to case work. Save the query when the same question will need to be revisited as additional permitted records become available.',
'12-explore-results': 'Enter the query and choose the relevant object type. Select a result to inspect its properties, relationships and source details. Where permitted, add the record to the active case or request a protected value with a reason. If continued review is required, save the query with an explanatory reason so subsequent matching records can be assessed in the same investigative context.',
'13-studio-cleanup': 'Review the suggested recipe and inspect its effect on representative values. Apply the appropriate steps in order, checking changed cells, duplicates and quarantined material. Reset the recipe where the transformation is unsuitable, then re-examine the result. Continue to field mapping once the prepared representation reflects the intended meaning of the source.',
'13-studio-fields': 'Review the dataset type and the proposed mapping of rows and columns. Confirm the properties and joins that will represent the source, then apply the mapping. Inspect fields that remain unmapped or are blocked by policy. The later changes preview should be checked to ensure that these choices produce the intended objects and relationships.',
'13-studio-matching': 'Filter the prepared rows by On record, Probable or New and examine the supporting identifiers. Open rows associated with a check where further context is required. Clear any temporary lens before returning to the complete extract. The matching result feeds the proposed operations, which the separate reviewer can inspect before publication.',
'13-studio-checks': 'Select a check and use Show rows to inspect the material that produced it. Compare the condition with its possible ordinary explanation, then record the appropriate checked or dismissed disposition. Examine the links and place views where useful. Proceed to the proposed changes only after the relevant issues and conflicts have been considered.',
'13-studio-changes': 'Review each operation category before submission. Compare incoming and existing values for additions, corroboration or conflicts, and inspect the records represented by proposed new objects. Check the order and explanatory note against that scope. The separate supervisor should receive a proposal whose actual effect can be understood without reconstructing the preparation process from the beginning.',
'13-studio-tagging': 'Read the original passage, inspect the highlighted references and select any linked identifier relevant to the enquiry. Compare the linked record with the surrounding wording before accepting the proposed association. Where a name is bound to an existing person, verify the supporting same-page identifier. The tagged reference then retains a route back to the location in the narrative that prompted it.',
'13-studio-diary': 'Review the document and its highlighted spans together. Open a linked identifier to inspect the available record and compare it with the written context. Where the document contains several similar names or references, preserve the exact span supporting the association. The proposal should reflect what the text supports rather than a broader inferred account of the document’s meaning.',
'13-studio-proposed': 'Check the prepared rows and operations, then submit the proposal with its legal order and explanatory note. Open Proposals in my cases to inspect the submitted state. The proposer waits for the separate eligible supervisor’s decision rather than approving the intake themselves. The frozen operations provide a stable subject for that review.',
'13-studio-review': 'Open the submitted proposal and compare its operations with the order and note. Enter the reason before selecting Approve and publish or Reject. If an already published intake requires reversal, inspect its recorded state and use Revert this publish with a reason. The available map and pattern actions provide context and follow-up around the publication decision.',
'13-studio-published': 'Open the completed proposal to inspect the approval and applied operations. Examine relevant resulting records in the case and determine whether additional analysis is needed. Re-run patterns where appropriate for the new material. If an error is identified, use the recorded revert workflow rather than treating publication as an untraceable edit to the source; the decision context remains available for review.',
'14-review': 'Select a waiting pair and compare its original fields side by side. Read the pairing explanation, then inspect agreements and differences in their source context. Enter a reason before choosing Same person or Different people. The decision records the reviewer’s interpretation of that pair and should be specific enough to support later reconsideration if source information changes.',
'14-review-pair': 'Start with the identifiers and distinguishing fields, then examine names, family information and district in context. Review why the system proposed the pair before making the human decision. Record the evidence relied upon in the reason field. Opening the comparison is an examination step; only the explicit decision applies the reviewed disposition.',
'15-faces-empty': 'Choose the probe image and confirm the preview. Enter the available order or seizure reference, then use Add as evidence and find faces. Inspect the detection result before continuing. If the image does not produce a usable detection, provide a more suitable source frame through the same evidence-led process rather than moving directly to an unsupported candidate search.',
'15-faces-found': 'Inspect the detected faces on the registered still and select the subject relevant to the case. Enter the search reason and run the comparison for that selection. Review the returned photographs as candidates with source context. The selected region and evidence image remain important references when another officer later considers whether a proposed match should be confirmed or rejected.',
'15-faces-candidates': 'Review each relevant candidate’s photograph, similarity and source, then inspect the visible contextual record where needed. Compare it with the selected probe rather than with another face in the original still. A proposed association proceeds to the pending review workflow for a different case officer. The searching officer should preserve the comparison context while the separate reviewer records the reasoned confirmation or rejection.',
'15-faces-confirm': 'Open a pending candidate as an eligible second officer of the case. Examine the probe, candidate photograph, similarity and available source information. Enter the decision reason and choose Confirm or Reject. Review the resulting status so the team can distinguish a pending suggestion from a completed human assessment. The original searching officer remains excluded from confirming their own search.',
'16-cop': 'Select an alert from the list and review its sender, timing and severity. Examine the map placement and the basis used to derive its affected area. Open the relevant district, hospital or gauge context where available, and review any unmapped entries through the list. If an allocation is made, record it through the dispatch workflow and inspect the resulting log.',
'16-cop-flood': 'Open the flood alert and compare its effective period with the available gauge and affected-district information. Review hospital references relevant to the area. If resources are allocated, choose the type, quantity and destination in the dispatch form. Check the resulting log entry against the intended response rather than assuming the form itself establishes physical delivery.',
'16-cop-dispatch': 'Review the selected alert context before opening the dispatch form. Choose the resource, enter its quantity and select the destination district. Submit the allocation and inspect the log for the intended entry. The recorded details support coordination and later review, while any external communication, fulfilment and confirmation remain part of the institution’s separate response arrangements.',
'17-oversight': 'Check the chain status, then apply a user or record filter suited to the oversight question. Review the returned events and relevant alerts in context. Seal the daily root where required and arrange its separate independent retention. Use the transparency view for aggregate information and the retention panel for case-lifecycle review.',
'17-oversight-filtered': 'Enter a user identifier, a record identifier or both and apply the filters. Read the returned events in time order with their purpose and outcome. Distinguish successful actions from refusals before assessing the activity. Check the chain state as part of the review and preserve an independent root reference through the separate operational process where required.',
'17-transparency': 'Review the activity aggregates by their selected dimensions, then inspect the retention categories available to the acting role. Distinguish cases that are scheduled, due or held. An authorised purge action should be followed by review of its recorded result and manifest reference. This makes the completed lifecycle operation distinguishable from a case merely appearing as eligible.',
'18-admin-library': 'Open the relevant detector entry and review its question, method, scope and version. Read the possible evasion, ordinary explanations and blind spots before comparing the documentation with a case finding. Use the source-quality register where the underlying material appears inconsistent. Diagnostic tabs serve maintainer review and are described without reproducing their test results here.',
'18-admin-quality': 'Locate the issue and note the affected record and originating source. Inspect the corresponding extract to establish the problem and its likely scope. Coordinate a correction with the source owner using those references, then assess any subsequent intake through the normal review process. The register records the issue for investigation; it does not silently apply changes to the source material.'
}
